{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Wrote the deterministic prefix the @joint_apply_order discriminator asked for and ran it locally, which resolves the apply-side ordering cleanly. The new case builds one voter with newTestRaft over withPeers(1,2,3) and drives the target's own applyConfChange: a JointImplicit learner-add leaves the tracker joint (voters still [1,2,3]) with AutoLeave true; applying a second joint-entering change while joint panics with exactly 'config is already joint'; applying the leave-joint change (an empty ConfChangeV2) returns the tracker to non-joint; and the same joint-entering change is then accepted with no panic, ending with learners [4,5]. That confirms the contract's ordering requirement at the apply side and shows the earlier harness panic was a property of the change/state pair rather than of some hidden defect: any party that applies a joint-entering change while its tracker is joint is rejected by the Changer, and the way through is to apply the leave-joint entry that precedes it, which is what the leader's own appliedTo path proposes. It does not by itself show that a permitted history forces such a change onto an in-order application, so the investigation stays at that limit. Because the result is draft-side only, the submission requests isolated execution of the harness with this deterministic case added, describing the prefix, the recover-guarded panic capture, the single CA_EVENT operands (joint flag after the first change, AutoLeave, voters after the first change, both panic texts, joint flag after the leave, final voter and learner sets), and the assumption that the case documents the ordering requirement rather than establishing reachability; probes A-C and the sweep are unchanged.\"}"}}
